{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are mildly high at short horizons (≈D+1 to D+10, typically +0.3 to +1.5 €/t) and become increasingly low at longer horizons when the market trends up (≈D+20 to D+30, often −1 to −3 €/t). Apply a bias schedule that shifts from slight negative adjustment early to positive adjustment late in rising regimes.",
    "Over-smoothing / lag: the forecast path is too flat and reacts slowly to turning points; it misses the depth of drawdowns and the strength of subsequent rebounds. Increase responsiveness to recent momentum (e.g., weight last 5–10 days more) and reduce reliance on older history when trend strength is high.",
    "Spike overshoot: when projecting a late-horizon rally, the model tends to overshoot local peaks (notably around D+26 to D+30 in several examples) and then mis-time the pullback. Cap late-horizon daily slope and damp projected peaks by ~1–2 €/t when the forecast implies a rapid multi-day acceleration.",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: after sharp declines, the model underestimates the speed/extent of mean reversion (stays too low around the trough window). Add a rebound kicker: if the last 10-day move is strongly negative, lift D+10 to D+20 levels modestly (≈+0.5 to +1.5 €/t).",
    "Regime check (trend vs range): errors are smaller in range-bound periods but widen in trending periods. Use a simple regime filter (e.g., 10–20d trend/volatility ratio): in trending-up regimes, tilt the whole curve upward and reduce peak overshoot; in trending-down regimes, reduce early-horizon high bias."
  ]
}
